\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 1 --- System Setup}}

The Trusted Authority (TA) initializes the global
cryptographic parameters and securely provisions the
required credentials to all system entities. Let
\(\lambda\) denote the security parameter,
\(\mathcal U\) the attribute universe, and
\(\mathcal D=\{D_1,\ldots,D_d\}\) the searchable numerical
dimensions.

\paragraph*{Step 1: ABSE and Selector Initialization}

The TA initializes the attribute-based searchable
encryption (ABSE) scheme

\begin{equation}
(PP,MSK)
\leftarrow
\textsf{ABSE.Setup}(1^\lambda),
\end{equation}

where \(PP\) contains the BN254 bilinear pairing
parameters
\((p,\mathbb G_1,\mathbb G_T,e,g)\),
providing approximately 128-bit security.

For each authorized user with attribute set
\(\mathcal A\subseteq\mathcal U\), the TA generates

\begin{equation}
SK_{\mathcal A}
\leftarrow
\textsf{ABSE.KeyGen}(MSK,\mathcal A).
\end{equation}

The TA further samples a long-term user selector secret

\begin{equation}
K_s
\xleftarrow{\$}
\{0,1\}^{\lambda},
\end{equation}

from which each authorized user derives the epoch-specific
selector key for the current query epoch \(e_q\):

\begin{equation}
K_s'
=
H(K_s\parallel e_q).
\end{equation}

The selector secret
\(K_s\) is used only during trapdoor generation and is
never disclosed to the Edge Gateway or the cloud.

\paragraph*{Step 2: Threshold Homomorphic Encryption Initialization}

The TA initializes lifted Threshold EC-ElGamal over the
NIST P-256 elliptic curve by sampling

\[
x
\xleftarrow{\$}
\mathbb Z_p^*,
\]

computing the public key

\[
pk_{AHE}
=
P
=
xG,
\]

and constructing the Shamir polynomial

\[
f(z)
=
x
+
\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}
a_i z^i,
\]

where
\(a_i\xleftarrow{\$}\mathbb Z_p^*\).

The corresponding secret shares

\[
sk_\ell
=
f(\ell),
\qquad
\ell=1,\ldots,n,
\]

are distributed to the Threshold Decryption Authorities
(TDAs), enabling any \(t\) of \(n\) TDAs to jointly decrypt
an aggregate ciphertext.

Each numerical value is encrypted as

\begin{equation}
CT_v
=
(rG,\,
vG+rP),
\qquad
r
\xleftarrow{\$}
\mathbb Z_p^*,
\end{equation}

which satisfies

\[
\textsf{Enc}(v_1)
+
\textsf{Enc}(v_2)
=
\textsf{Enc}(v_1+v_2).
\]

\paragraph*{Step 3: Cryptographic Primitive Initialization}

The TA initializes the pseudorandom function
\(F\), hash-to-group function
\(H_g\), cryptographic hash
\(H\), key-derivation function
\(\textsf{KDF}\), and digital signature scheme

\[
\mathsf{DS}
=
(
\mathsf{KeyGen},
\mathsf{Sign},
\mathsf{Verify}
),
\]

where
\(F\) is instantiated using HMAC-SHA-256,
\(H_g\) over BN254,
\(H\) using SHA-256,
\(\textsf{KDF}\) using HKDF-SHA-256,
and
\(\mathsf{DS}\)
using ECDSA over secp256k1.

\paragraph*{Step 4: Credential Provisioning}

For each sensor \(i\), the TA derives

\[
K_{AES}^{(i)}
=
\textsf{KDF}(K_{master},ID_i),
\]

and

\[
K_{HMAC}^{(i)}
=
\textsf{KDF}
(K_{master},
ID_i\parallel\texttt{"hmac"}).
\]

The TA also samples the bitmap-masking key

\[
K_{sel}
\xleftarrow{\$}
\{0,1\}^{\lambda},
\]

and generates the Edge Gateway signing key pair

\begin{equation}
(pk_{DO},sk_{DO})
\leftarrow
\mathsf{DS.KeyGen}(1^\lambda).
\end{equation}

Sensors receive
\((K_{AES}^{(i)},K_{HMAC}^{(i)},pk_{AHE})\),
authorized users receive
\((SK_{\mathcal A},K_s,K_{sel})\),
the Edge Gateway receives
\((K_{sel},sk_{DO})\),
and each TDA receives one threshold share
\(sk_\ell\).

The long-term selector secret \(K_s\) is used only by
authorized users to derive epoch-specific query selectors,
whereas the bitmap-masking key \(K_{sel}\) is shared
between authorized users and the Edge Gateway to construct
and recover protected bitmaps during authenticated query
processing.

\paragraph*{Step 5: Blockchain Initialization}

The genesis commitment
\(Root_0\)
is anchored on the blockchain as the initial authenticated
system state. Finally, the TA publishes

\[
(PP,\,
pk_{AHE},\,
pk_{DO},\,
\{pk_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^{n}),
\]

while

\[
(MSK,\,
K_s,\,
K_{sel},\,
K_{master},\,
sk_{DO},\,
\{sk_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^{n})
\]

remain confidential.

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 2 --- Sensor-Side Encryption and Edge-Side Authenticated Index Construction}}

This phase transforms sensor readings into authenticated
searchable structures while preserving confidentiality. The
resulting index supports conjunctive range search,
encrypted aggregation, and blockchain-verifiable integrity.

\paragraph*{Step 1: Sensor-Side Encryption}

For each sensor record

\[
D_i=(m_i,k_i,t_i,v_i),
\]

the sensor locally derives the canonical node identifier
\(u_j\) for each searchable dimension \(D_j\) according to
the public canonical range-cover tree and constructs

\[
Meta_i=\{(D_j,u_j)\}_{j=1}^{d}.
\]

The complete record is encrypted using AES-GCM to obtain
\(CT_{AES}^{(i)}\), while the numerical value is encrypted
using Threshold EC-ElGamal to produce \(CT_v^{(i)}\).
The sensor then forms

\[
\mathcal P_i=
(ID_i,seq_i,N_i,CT_{AES}^{(i)},CT_v^{(i)},Meta_i,Tag_i),
\]

where

\[
\begin{split}
Tag_i=
\textsf{HMAC}_{K_{HMAC}^{(i)}}
( & ID_i\parallel seq_i\parallel N_i \\
& \parallel CT_{AES}^{(i)}
\parallel CT_v^{(i)}
\parallel Meta_i),
\end{split}
\]

and transmits \(\mathcal P_i\) to the Edge Gateway.
Consequently, canonical nodes are derived only by the sensor,
whereas the gateway constructs the authenticated searchable
index using the authenticated metadata \(Meta_i\) without
decrypting the outsourced data.

\paragraph*{Step 2: Edge-Side Verification}

Upon receiving \(\mathcal P_i\), the gateway verifies the
HMAC and freshness condition
\(seq_i>seq_i^{last}\).
If valid, it checks that every
\((D_j,u_j)\in Meta_i\)
corresponds to a legitimate searchable dimension and
canonical node, updates
\(seq_i^{last}\leftarrow seq_i\),
and forwards
\((CT_{AES}^{(i)},CT_v^{(i)},Meta_i)\)
for authenticated index construction.

\paragraph*{Step 3: Searchable Node Construction}

For each canonical node \(u\) in dimension \(D_j\), let
\(l_u\) and \(r_u\) denote the left and right range boundaries
of~\(u\). The gateway computes

\[
\tau_u^{struct}=H_g(l_u\parallel r_u),
\]

\[
\tau_u^{ctx}=F(K_{sel},D_j\parallel u),
\]

\[
\tau_u=H_g(\tau_u^{struct}\parallel\tau_u^{ctx}),
\]

constructs the protected bitmap

\[
\widetilde B_u=
B_u\oplus
F(K_{sel},e\parallel D_j\parallel u),
\]

computes

\[
\sigma_u^{bmp}=H(u\parallel\widetilde B_u),
\]

encrypts the searchable tag

\[
CT_u^{tag}
=
\mathsf{ABSE.Enc}(PP,\tau_u,\mathcal P),
\]

and finally generates the authenticated searchable-node
commitment

\[
\sigma_u=
H(u\parallel CT_u^{tag}
\parallel\sigma_u^{bmp}\parallel e).
\]

All bitmaps are aligned over the global record space,
enabling efficient bitwise conjunction during conjunctive
range evaluation.

\paragraph*{Step 4: Aggregation Metadata Registration}

For each record, the gateway registers

\[
\mathcal A_i=
(rid_i,CT_v^{(i)},Enc^{AHE}_{pk_{AHE}}(1)),
\]

ensuring that each matched record contributes exactly once
to encrypted aggregation.

\paragraph*{Step 5: Authenticated Commitment Construction}

At the end of epoch \(e\), the gateway computes

\[
Root_e^{idx},
\quad
Root_e^{agg},
\quad
Root_e^{bmp},
\]

as the Merkle roots of
\(\{\sigma_u\}\),
\(\{\mathcal A_i\}\),
and
\(\{\sigma_u^{bmp}\}\),
respectively, and derives

\[
Root_e=
H(Root_e^{idx}\parallel
Root_e^{agg}\parallel
Root_e^{bmp}\parallel e).
\]

It then computes

\[
m_e=H(Root_e\parallel ts),
\]

signs

\[
\sigma_{DO}=
\mathsf{DS.Sign}_{sk_{DO}}(m_e),
\]

and anchors the authenticated epoch state

\[
(e,Root_e,ts,\sigma_{DO})
\]

on the blockchain.

\paragraph*{Step 6: Secure Outsourcing}

Finally, the gateway outsources

\[
\{
CT_{AES}^{(i)},
CT_u^{tag},
\widetilde B_u,
\mathcal A_i,
\sigma_u,
\sigma_u^{bmp}
\}
\]

to the cloud. During authorized query processing, the cloud
recovers protected bitmaps using the selector-derived masks
without learning plaintext sensor values.


\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 3 --- Conjunctive Range-Query Trapdoor Generation}}

An authorized user possessing attribute key
\(SK_{\mathcal A}\) locally generates a conjunctive
range-query trapdoor without revealing the queried
dimensions or numerical ranges.

\paragraph*{Step 1: Canonical Range Decomposition}

A conjunctive query is defined as

\begin{equation}
Q
=
\bigwedge_{j=1}^{d}
(D_j,[a_j,b_j]).
\end{equation}

For each searchable dimension \(D_j\), the user
decomposes the query interval into the minimal canonical
range-cover set

\begin{equation}
\mathcal C_j
=
\{u_{j,1},\ldots,u_{j,\ell_j}\},
\qquad
\ell_j
=
O(\log |D_j|).
\end{equation}

\paragraph*{Step 2: Search Token Generation}

For every canonical node
\(u_{j,i}\in\mathcal C_j\), the user reconstructs the
searchable tag

\begin{equation}
\tau_{u_{j,i}}
=
H_g
\!\left(
H_g(l_u\!\parallel\! r_u)
\parallel
F(K_{sel},D_j\!\parallel\!u_{j,i})
\right),
\end{equation}

randomizes it as

\begin{equation}
\tau'_{u_{j,i}}
=
\tau_{u_{j,i}}
g^{r_{j,i}},
\qquad
r_{j,i}
\xleftarrow{\$}
\mathbb Z_p,
\end{equation}

and generates the attribute-bound search token

\begin{equation}
Tok_{j,i}
=
\textsf{ABSE.TokenGen}
(
SK_{\mathcal A},
\tau'_{u_{j,i}}
).
\end{equation}

\paragraph*{Step 3: Node-Selector Generation}

For each canonical node, the user derives the
epoch-dependent bitmap selector

\begin{equation}
Sel_{j,i}
=
F
(
K_{sel},
e
\parallel
D_j
\parallel
u_{j,i}
),
\end{equation}

where \(e\) denotes the latest finalized index epoch.
The selector enables recovery of only the matched bitmap

\begin{equation}
B_u
=
\widetilde B_u
\oplus
Sel_{j,i},
\end{equation}

without revealing the secret selector seed
\(K_{sel}\).

\paragraph*{Step 4: Trapdoor Construction}

The final query trapdoor is

\begin{equation}
T_Q
=
\Bigl\{
(
Tok_{j,i},
g^{r_{j,i}},
Sel_{j,i}
)
\Bigr\},
\qquad
u_{j,i}\in\mathcal C_j,
\end{equation}

whose size is

\begin{equation}
|T_Q|
=
O
\!\left(
\sum_{j=1}^{d}
\log |D_j|
\right).
\end{equation}

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 4 --- Verifiable Conjunctive Range Search and Aggregation}}

Using the query trapdoor \(T_Q\), the cloud performs
authorized searchable matching, bitmap recovery,
conjunctive range evaluation, encrypted aggregation, and
verifiable proof generation without learning the plaintext
query, bitmap-selector secret, or sensor data.

\paragraph*{Step 1: Trapdoor Evaluation and Node Matching}

For each canonical node \(u_i\) produced by the
deterministic range-cover decomposition of the query
interval over dimension \(D_j\), the authorized user
constructs one trapdoor component

\[
T_{j,i}
=
(
Tok_{j,i},
\tau_{u_i}^{ctx},
Mask_{j,i}
),
\]

where

\[
Mask_{j,i}
=
F(K_{sel},
e
\parallel
D_j
\parallel
u_i),
\]

and

\[
\tau_{u_i}^{ctx}
=
F(K_{sel},
D_j
\parallel
u_i).
\]

The complete trapdoor is

\[
T_Q
=
\{T_{j,i}\}.
\]

The cloud receives only the ephemeral node-specific masks
and context identifiers; it never learns the bitmap-selector
secret \(K_{sel}\).

For every trapdoor component \(T_{j,i}\), the cloud
evaluates

\[
\mathsf{ABSE.Match}
(
Tok_{j,i},
CT_u^{tag}
),
\]

and retrieves the unique authenticated searchable node

\[
(
\widetilde B_u,
\sigma_u,
\sigma_u^{bmp},
\mathcal A_i
)
\]

whose encrypted tag matches the supplied search token.
Because each trapdoor component corresponds to exactly one
canonical node, the associated bitmap mask
\(Mask_{j,i}\) is uniquely bound to the recovered bitmap.

\paragraph*{Step 2: Bitmap Recovery and Conjunctive Evaluation}

For each matched canonical node, the cloud recovers the
corresponding bitmap by removing the node-specific mask
contained in the authorized trapdoor:

\begin{equation}
B_u
=
\widetilde B_u
\oplus
Mask_{j,i}.
\end{equation}

The cloud never evaluates the underlying PRF nor derives
the secret bitmap-selector seed \(K_{sel}\); it simply
applies the authorized mask provided in the trapdoor.

Since all bitmaps are aligned over the same global record
space, the cloud computes the dimension-level bitmap

\begin{equation}
B_{D_j}
=
\bigvee_{u\in\mathcal M_j}
B_u,
\end{equation}

followed by the conjunctive bitmap

\begin{equation}
B_Q
=
\bigwedge_{j=1}^{d}
B_{D_j}.
\end{equation}

The resulting matched record set is

\begin{equation}
\mathcal R_Q
=
\{
r_i
\mid
B_Q[i]=1
\},
\end{equation}

while

\begin{equation}
\mathcal U_Q
=
\bigcup_{j=1}^{d}
\mathcal M_j
\end{equation}

denotes the authenticated canonical-node set retained for
response verification.

Because the canonical range-cover decomposition is
deterministic, each recovered bitmap is uniquely associated
with an authenticated canonical node. Consequently,
\(B_Q\) deterministically identifies the record set
corresponding to the authenticated query state. The
returned bitmap is subsequently authenticated through the
Merkle proofs and blockchain-anchored query-state
commitments during response verification.

\paragraph*{Step 3: Bitmap-Aware Encrypted Aggregation}

Using the matched record set
\(\mathcal R_Q\) derived from the conjunctive bitmap
\(B_Q\), the cloud computes

\[
CT_{sum}
=
\bigoplus_{r_i\in\mathcal R_Q}
CT_v^{(i)},
\]

and

\[
CT_{count}
=
\bigoplus_{r_i\in\mathcal R_Q}
Enc^{AHE}_{pk_{AHE}}(1).
\]

Since \(\mathcal R_Q\) is constructed from the
conjunctive bitmap, each record identifier contributes at
most once to the aggregation, thereby avoiding duplicate
counting caused by overlapping canonical nodes. The
resulting aggregation ciphertexts remain encrypted under
the Threshold EC-ElGamal public key and are
cryptographically bound to the authenticated query state
through the response commitment
\(\Pi_{batch}\). After successful response verification,
the aggregate ciphertexts are jointly decrypted by at
least \(t\) Threshold Decryption Authorities.
\paragraph*{Step 4: Verifiable Response Construction}

For every
\(u\in\mathcal U_Q\), the cloud generates the searchable-node
Merkle proof

\[
\pi_u
=
\textsf{MerkleProof}
(
\sigma_u,
Root_e^{idx}
),
\]

and the bitmap-commitment Merkle proof

\[
\pi_u^{bmp}
=
\textsf{MerkleProof}
(
\sigma_u^{bmp},
Root_e^{bmp}
).
\]

The cloud then computes the authenticated batch commitment

\[
\begin{aligned}
\Pi_{batch}
=
H(
&
Acc_e
\parallel
Root_e^{agg}
\\
&
\parallel
H(\{\sigma_u\})
\parallel
H(\{\sigma_u^{bmp}\})
\\
&
\parallel
H(B_Q)
\parallel
CT_{sum}
\parallel
CT_{count}
).
\end{aligned}
\]

The batch commitment cryptographically binds the returned
canonical nodes, bitmap commitments, conjunctive bitmap,
aggregation ciphertexts, and blockchain-authenticated
query state into a single authenticated response.
Consequently, any modification, substitution, omission, or
replay of these components changes
\(\Pi_{batch}\) or the corresponding Merkle proofs and is
detected during response verification. The authenticated
response guarantees the integrity and consistency of the
returned query state with respect to the blockchain-anchored
commitments; it does not constitute a general proof of
bitmap evaluation or homomorphic aggregation.

Finally, the cloud returns

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal Rsp_Q
=
\Bigl(
&
CT_{sum},
CT_{count},
B_Q,
\Pi_{batch},
\\
&
\{\sigma_u\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
\{\sigma_u^{bmp}\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
\\
&
\{\pi_u\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
\{\pi_u^{bmp}\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
Root_e
\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The response contains the aggregation ciphertexts,
authenticated bitmap state, Merkle authentication proofs,
and the blockchain-anchored epoch commitment required for
verification. If record retrieval is requested, the
corresponding encrypted records

\[
CT_{AES}^{(i)},
\qquad
r_i\in\mathcal R_Q,
\]

are also returned.

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 5 --- Blockchain-Anchored Verification and Threshold Decryption}}

Upon receiving

\[
\begin{aligned}
Rsp_Q
=
\Bigl(
&
e,
CT_{sum},
CT_{count},
B_Q,
\mathcal R_Q,
\Pi_{batch},
\\
&
\{\sigma_u\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
\{\sigma_u^{bmp}\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
\\
&
\{\pi_u\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
\{\pi_u^{bmp}\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
Root_e,
\{CT_{K_i}\}
\Bigr),
\end{aligned}
\]

the user first verifies the authenticity, integrity,
completeness, and freshness of the returned query result
before performing threshold aggregate decryption or
optional record decryption.

\paragraph*{Step 1: Blockchain and Query-State Verification}

The user retrieves the authenticated state

\[
State_e
=
(
e,
Root_e^{idx},
Root_e^{agg},
Root_e^{bmp},
Root_e,
ts,
\sigma_{DO}
)
\]

from the blockchain, verifies

\begin{equation}
\mathsf{DS.Verify}_{pk_{DO}}
\bigl(
H(Root_e\parallel ts),
\sigma_{DO}
\bigr)
=
1,
\end{equation}

and confirms that \(e\) is the latest finalized epoch.
The user reconstructs the canonical range cover from the
query trapdoor, verifies that the returned canonical-node
set matches the expected decomposition, validates all
Merkle proofs against
\(Root_e^{idx}\)
and
\(Root_e^{bmp}\),
and recomputes

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Pi_{batch}^{*}
=
H\Bigl(
&
Root_e
\parallel
H(\{\sigma_u\})
\\
&
\parallel
H(\{\sigma_u^{bmp}\})
\parallel
H(B_Q)
\\
&
\parallel
H(\mathcal R_Q)
\parallel
CT_{sum}
\parallel
CT_{count}
\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The response is accepted for decryption only if

\[
\Pi_{batch}^{*}
=
\Pi_{batch}.
\]
Successful verification confirms the authenticity,
integrity, completeness, and freshness of the authenticated
query state, and cryptographically binds the returned
aggregation ciphertexts
\((CT_{sum},CT_{count})\)
to the verified bitmap
\(B_Q\)
and matched record set
\(\mathcal R_Q\).
\paragraph*{Step 2: Threshold Aggregate Decryption}

After successful verification, the user submits
\((CT_{sum},CT_{count})\) to a set
\(S\) of Threshold Decryption Authorities satisfying
\(|S|\ge t\). Each authority
\(\ell\in S\) computes

\[
\delta_\ell^{sum}
=
sk_\ell C_1^{sum},
\qquad
\delta_\ell^{count}
=
sk_\ell C_1^{count},
\]

which are verified using the corresponding public
verification keys. The user reconstructs

\[
D_{sum}
=
\sum_{\ell\in S}
\lambda_\ell
\delta_\ell^{sum},
\qquad
D_{count}
=
\sum_{\ell\in S}
\lambda_\ell
\delta_\ell^{count},
\]

and recovers

\[
M_{sum}
=
C_2^{sum}
-
D_{sum},
\qquad
M_{count}
=
C_2^{count}
-
D_{count}.
\]

Finally,

\[
Sum
=
\mathsf{BSGS}(M_{sum}),
\qquad
Count
=
\mathsf{BSGS}(M_{count}),
\]

and

\[
Avg
=
\frac{Sum}{Count},
\qquad
Count>0.
\]

\paragraph*{Step 3: Optional Record Decryption}

During data outsourcing, the Edge Gateway generates an
ABSE-protected key envelope

\[
CT_{K_i}
=
\textsf{ABSE.Enc}
(PP,
K_{AES}^{(i)},
\mathcal P).
\]

For record-retrieval queries, each returned
\(CT_{AES}^{(i)}\) is accompanied by
\(CT_{K_i}\). A user whose attributes satisfy
\(\mathcal P\) recovers
\(K_{AES}^{(i)}\) and decrypts

\[
D_i
=
\textsf{AES\mbox{-}GCM.Dec}
(K_{AES}^{(i)},
CT_{AES}^{(i)}).
\]

The AES-GCM authentication tag provides end-to-end record
integrity and authenticity. The query result is accepted
only if blockchain verification, canonical-cover
verification, Merkle-proof validation, authenticated batch
verification, and the requested decryption operation(s)
complete successfully; otherwise, the response is rejected.

\section{Security Analysis}

\subsection{Security Model}

We consider a probabilistic polynomial-time (PPT)
adversary \(\mathcal A\) that controls the cloud server
and communication channels. During normal protocol
execution, the Edge Gateway is assumed to be
honest-but-curious and faithfully performs sensor
authentication, authenticated index construction,
aggregation-metadata generation, and blockchain
commitment, while attempting to infer information only
from the permitted leakage.

The adversary observes encrypted records, searchable
ciphertexts, protected bitmaps, aggregation metadata,
query trapdoors, Merkle proofs, authenticated batch
commitments, blockchain states, and the leakage function
\(\mathcal L_{BVCRSA}\). It may adaptively issue
ciphertext, trapdoor, aggregation, and verification
queries, and may attempt to infer plaintext information
beyond the permitted leakage, generate unauthorized
trapdoors, omit, modify, replay, or fabricate query
responses, or forge verification proofs.

The adversary does not possess the ABSE master secret
\(MSK\), the long-term bitmap-selector secret
\(K_s\), the Edge Gateway signing key
\(sk_{DO}\), any unauthorized attribute secret key, or
the threshold decryption shares
\(\{sk_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^{n}\). The query-specific selector
key

\[
K_s'
=
H(K_s\parallel e_q)
\]

is deterministically derived only by authorized users
during trapdoor generation and is never disclosed to the
Edge Gateway or the cloud. We assume that fewer than
\(t\) Threshold Decryption Authorities (TDAs) may be
corrupted; consequently, the threshold decryption key
cannot be reconstructed and aggregation ciphertexts
remain confidential. Detecting or tolerating a malicious
Edge Gateway that intentionally anchors incorrect
authenticated states before blockchain commitment is
outside the scope of this work.

The security objective of BVCRSA is to guarantee

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&
\mathsf{Privacy}
\wedge
\mathsf{Confidentiality}
\wedge
\mathsf{AntiCollusion}
\\
&
{}\wedge
\mathsf{QueryStateIntegrity}
\wedge
\mathsf{QueryStateCompleteness}
\wedge
\mathsf{Freshness},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

while revealing only the leakage explicitly specified by
\(\mathcal L_{BVCRSA}\). In particular, BVCRSA
cryptographically authenticates that the returned
aggregation ciphertexts correspond to the verified
matched record set and authenticated query state, but it
does not claim general verifiable computation for
arbitrary homomorphic operations.
\subsection{Leakage Function}

BVCRSA follows the standard adaptive searchable-encryption
leakage model and reveals only the structural information
necessary for encrypted query processing.

The overall leakage profile is defined as

\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{BVCRSA}
=
\Bigl(
\mathcal L_{\mathsf{Setup}},
\mathcal L_{\mathsf{Data}},
\mathcal L_{\mathsf{Query}},
\mathcal L_{\mathsf{Access}}
\Bigr).
\end{equation}

\paragraph*{Setup Leakage.}

The setup phase reveals only the public parameters

\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathsf{Setup}}
=
PP.
\end{equation}

No master secret keys, attribute secret keys, aggregation
private keys, bitmap-selector seed, or signing keys are
disclosed.

\paragraph*{Data Leakage.}

For each outsourced record, the cloud learns

\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathsf{Data}}
=
\Bigl(
|CT_{AES}|,
|CT_v|,
|\mathcal P_v|
\Bigr),
\end{equation}

where \(|CT_{AES}|\) and \(|CT_v|\) denote the ciphertext
sizes and \(|\mathcal P_v|\) denotes the number of canonical
range-cover nodes associated with the record.

\paragraph*{Query Leakage.}

For each query, the cloud learns

\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathsf{Query}}
=
\Bigl(
|T_Q|,
Pat_Q
\Bigr),
\end{equation}

where \(Pat_Q\) denotes the search pattern indicating whether
two trapdoors generate identical matched-node sets.

\paragraph*{Access Leakage.}

During query execution, the cloud observes

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L_{\mathsf{Access}}
=
\Bigl(
&
\mathcal U_Q,
\{B_u\},
B_Q,
\\
&
\mathcal R_Q,
|\mathcal R_Q|
\Bigr),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where

\begin{itemize}
\item \(\mathcal U_Q\) denotes the matched canonical-node set;
\item \(\{B_u\}\) denotes the recovered canonical-node bitmaps;
\item \(B_Q\) denotes the conjunctive query bitmap;
\item \(\mathcal R_Q\) denotes the matched record set;
\item \(|\mathcal R_Q|\) denotes the query-result cardinality.
\end{itemize}

For consistency with standard searchable-encryption
terminology, we define

\begin{equation}
\mathsf{SearchPattern}
=
Pat_Q,
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathsf{AccessPattern}
=
\Bigl(
\mathcal U_Q,
\mathcal R_Q
\Bigr),
\end{equation}

and

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathsf{SizePattern}
=
\Bigl(
&
|CT_{AES}|,
|CT_v|,
|\mathcal P_v|,
\\
&
|T_Q|,
|\mathcal U_Q|,
|\mathcal R_Q|
\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Accordingly,

\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{BVCRSA}
=
\Bigl(
PP,
\mathsf{SizePattern},
\mathsf{SearchPattern},
\mathsf{AccessPattern}
\Bigr),
\end{equation}

or equivalently,

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L_{BVCRSA}
=
\Bigl(
&
PP,
|CT_{AES}|,
|CT_v|,
|\mathcal P_v|,
\\
&
|T_Q|,
Pat_Q,
\mathcal U_Q,
\{B_u\},
\\
&
B_Q,
\mathcal R_Q,
|\mathcal R_Q|
\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Beyond the information explicitly captured by
\(\mathcal L_{BVCRSA}\), BVCRSA reveals no additional
information regarding plaintext sensor records, plaintext
numerical values, queried numerical ranges, searchable
dimensions, homomorphic decryption results, bitmap-selector
secrets, aggregation private keys, or authenticated
query-state signing keys.

The leakage specified by
\(\mathcal L_{BVCRSA}\)
reflects the practical tradeoff adopted by most
searchable-encryption schemes. An honest-but-curious cloud
may correlate repeated or overlapping queries through the
revealed matched canonical nodes, bitmap intersections,
accessed records, and result cardinality, thereby inferring
coarse information such as query selectivity or data
distribution. Representative searchable-encryption schemes
(e.g., Refs.~\cite{ref26,ref27,ref28,ref16}) likewise reveal
search and access patterns, although the exact leakage
profiles differ according to their constructions. BVCRSA
further reveals authenticated canonical-node and bitmap
information to enable query-state completeness verification
and encrypted aggregation without relying on ORAM,
zero-knowledge proofs, or trusted hardware.

\subsection{Theorem 1: Conjunctive Query Privacy}

\textbf{Theorem 1.}
Assuming the adaptive security of ABSE and the
pseudorandomness of
\(F(K_s',\cdot)\), BVCRSA preserves conjunctive query
privacy against probabilistic polynomial-time adversaries,
revealing no information beyond the leakage function
\(\mathcal L_{BVCRSA}\).

\textbf{Proof.}

We prove the theorem through a sequence of games.

\textbf{Game \(G_0\).}
The adversary observes the real execution of BVCRSA,
including searchable ciphertexts, query tokens, node
selectors, authenticated batch commitments, Merkle proofs,
and blockchain states.

\textbf{Game \(G_1\).}
Replace each node selector

\begin{equation}
Sel_{j,i}
=
F
\bigl(
K_s',
D_j
\parallel
u_{j,i}
\bigr)
\end{equation}

with a uniformly random value of the same length.
Distinguishing \(G_1\) from \(G_0\) breaks the
pseudorandomness of
\(F(K_s',\cdot)\). Therefore,

\begin{equation}
\left|
Pr[G_1]-Pr[G_0]
\right|
\le
Adv_{\mathcal A}^{PRF}(\lambda).
\end{equation}

\textbf{Game \(G_2\).}
Replace the ABSE searchable ciphertexts and query tokens
with simulated values generated solely from the leakage
