consists of two aggregate ciphertexts, one conjunctive
bitmap, authenticated commitments, and Merkle proofs for the
matched canonical nodes, resulting in
\(O(|B_Q|+|\mathcal U_Q|\log n_e)\) communication in
addition to two constant-size aggregate ciphertexts.
Unlike existing schemes that return all matched encrypted
records, the response size of BVCRSA is independent of the
number of matched ciphertexts for aggregation queries.
Consequently, BVCRSA achieves communication-efficient
encrypted aggregation while preserving authenticated
query-state verification.



\subsection{Storage Overhead Analysis}

BVCRSA stores encrypted sensor records, authenticated
range-cover indexes, protected bitmaps, aggregation
ciphertexts, and blockchain commitments. Let $N$ denote the
number of outsourced records and $m_c$ the total number of
canonical interval nodes. The encrypted sensor records incur
$O(N)$ storage, while the authenticated range-cover index
requires $O(m_c)$ searchable-node ciphertexts and Merkle-tree
leaf commitments. Protected bitmaps require
$O(m_cN)$ bits because each canonical node maintains a
fixed-length bitmap over the indexed record space.
Aggregation metadata require one EC-ElGamal ciphertext per
record, yielding $O(N)$ additional storage. Finally, the
blockchain stores only compact epoch-level commitments
consisting of authenticated Merkle roots and signatures,
resulting in $O(E)$ on-chain storage for $E$ finalized
epochs. Consequently, the storage overhead is dominated by
the bitmap index, while blockchain storage remains
independent of the outsourced dataset size.
\subsection{Performance Evaluation}

We evaluate BVCRSA in terms of efficiency, scalability,
privacy-preserving aggregation, and authenticated
verification over large-scale outsourced IIoT data. The
evaluation considers four aspects: (i) searchable-index
construction, (ii) trapdoor generation and conjunctive range
query processing, (iii) encrypted aggregation, and (iv)
blockchain-based query-state verification.

BVCRSA is compared with representative searchable
encryption and range-query schemes, including
Scheme~\cite{ref25}, Trinity~\cite{ref26},
ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27}, Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and
VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}. Since no existing scheme simultaneously supports
attribute-based searchable encryption, conjunctive range
queries, encrypted aggregation, authenticated
blockchain-backed verification, and threshold aggregate
decryption, only the functionalities shared by the
compared schemes (e.g., index construction, trapdoor
generation, query processing, and verification) are
evaluated comparatively. The encrypted aggregation
capability of BVCRSA, which is unsupported by the
baseline schemes, is evaluated separately through a
dedicated experiment. The threshold aggregate-decryption
cost is excluded from the comparative evaluation because
it is a post-processing operation not provided by the
baseline schemes.
\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experimental Setup}}

A BVCRSA prototype was implemented in Python~3.12 using
widely adopted open-source cryptographic libraries.
Attribute-based searchable encryption was realized with
BN128 bilinear pairings via \texttt{py\_ecc}, while
Threshold EC-ElGamal was implemented over the NIST P-256
elliptic curve. AES-GCM encryption was provided by
\texttt{PyCryptodome}, and SHA-256 and HMAC were
implemented using Python's native cryptographic libraries.
Blockchain-assisted query-state verification was realized
through Solidity smart contracts accessed via
\texttt{Web3.py}. All baseline schemes were reimplemented
by the authors according to their original algorithmic
descriptions and evaluated on the same hardware platform,
software environment, and comparable security settings to
ensure a fair comparison.

Experiments were conducted in a heterogeneous IIoT
environment comprising a Raspberry Pi~4 Model~B (4~GB RAM)
for sensor-side operations, an Intel Core~i7 laptop (16~GB
RAM) for trapdoor generation, and an Intel Xeon server
(32~GB RAM, Ubuntu~22.04~LTS) for index construction,
query processing, aggregation, and verification.

Synthetic IIoT datasets consisting of records
\(D_i=(m_i,k_i,t_i,v_i)\) were generated using 20 sensor categories,
with numerical values uniformly distributed over
\([0,100]\). The dataset size ranged from
\(10^3\) to \(2\times10^4\) records. Performance was
evaluated with respect to database size
(\(N=10^3\)--\(2\times10^4\)), query selectivity
(10\%--80\%), and the number of queried dimensions
(\(d=1\)--5). Measured metrics included index-construction time,
trapdoor generation, query processing, verification,
aggregation, and storage overhead. Unless otherwise
specified, all reported results represent the average of 20
independent runs under the same experimental
configuration. Since the observed run-to-run variance was
negligible, only the average values are reported.
BVCRSA targets heterogeneous IIoT deployments in which
lightweight sensors perform only data acquisition,
symmetric encryption, and authenticated metadata
generation, while computationally intensive operations,
including ABSE processing, authenticated index
construction, homomorphic aggregation, and query
verification, are offloaded to the Edge Gateway and cloud.
Accordingly, the evaluation emphasizes the cryptographic
performance and system-level scalability of verifiable
encrypted query processing rather than device-level
resource utilization or deployment cost.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Index Construction Time}}

This experiment evaluates the preprocessing overhead required to construct searchable indexes before outsourcing. The database size is varied from $1{,}000$ to $20{,}000$ records, while the number of searchable dimensions and access-policy size are fixed. Index-construction time includes encrypted node-tag generation, bitmap-commitment construction, aggregation-metadata initialization, and Merkle-root computation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{fig_index_construction.png}
\caption{Index-construction time versus database size.}
\label{fig:index_construction}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:index_construction} compares the index-construction overhead of BVCRSA with Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27}, Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}. As the database size increases, all schemes incur higher preprocessing costs due to the growing number of searchable index entries. Among the evaluated schemes, VC-KASE achieves the lowest index-construction cost because it maintains a relatively lightweight keyword-based indexing structure. BVCRSA incurs slightly higher overhead owing to the construction of authenticated range-cover nodes, protected bitmap commitments, encrypted aggregation metadata, and Merkle-based verification structures. Nevertheless, these operations are performed only once during data outsourcing and therefore do not affect online query execution. Compared with Trinity and Latt-IBEKS, BVCRSA exhibits substantially lower preprocessing overhead because its index construction primarily relies on efficient hash computations, PRF evaluations, and incremental EC-ElGamal aggregation updates rather than expensive multidimensional encryption or lattice-based operations. 

\subsubsection{\textbf{Trapdoor Generation Time}}

This experiment evaluates the client-side overhead of trapdoor generation. The number of queried dimensions is varied from 1 to 5 while the database size is fixed at $N=10{,}000$ records. Trapdoor-generation time is measured as the elapsed time required to construct a valid search trapdoor before query submission.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{fig_trapdoor_generation.png}
\caption{Trapdoor-generation time versus number of queried dimensions.}
\label{fig:trapdoor_generation}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:trapdoor_generation} compares the trapdoor-generation overhead of BVCRSA with Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27}, Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}. As the number of queried dimensions increases, all schemes incur additional cost due to the need to encode more query predicates.

BVCRSA exhibits only a modest increase in latency, from approximately $0.9$~ms to $2.2$~ms when the number of queried dimensions increases from 1 to 5. This efficiency arises from canonical interval decomposition combined with lightweight hash, PRF, and attribute-bound token-generation operations. Since each dimension contributes only a logarithmic number of interval nodes, the trapdoor cost scales gradually with query complexity.

In contrast, Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27}, Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} incur higher overhead due to additional cryptographic operations during token construction. Overall, the results demonstrate that BVCRSA generates compact and efficient trapdoors while preserving query privacy and fine-grained access control, making it well suited for multidimensional encrypted range queries in large-scale IIoT environments.





\subsubsection{\textbf{Query Processing Time}}

Query-processing latency is a critical performance metric for outsourced searchable-encryption systems because it directly determines the responsiveness of encrypted data retrieval and analytics. This experiment evaluates the query-processing performance of BVCRSA under varying query selectivities, database sizes, and numbers of queried dimensions. The reported query-processing time includes encrypted search, bitmap filtering, and homomorphic aggregation, but excludes network communication and blockchain-access latency to isolate the efficiency of the search framework itself.


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{fig_query_vs_range.png}
\caption{Query processing time versus range-query width.}
\label{fig:query_vs_range}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:query_vs_range} evaluates the impact of
range-query width on query-processing latency. As the
queried interval expands from $10\%$ to $80\%$ of the
searchable domain, BVCRSA maintains consistently low
latency, increasing only from approximately $0.04$~ms to
$0.20$~ms. In contrast, VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} and
Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28} require approximately $1$~ms,
whereas ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27} and Trinity~\cite{ref26}
incur approximately $40$~ms and $90$~ms, respectively.
BVCRSA achieves this advantage through logarithmic
canonical range-cover decomposition and efficient
word-parallel bitmap evaluation, whereas Trinity relies on
more computationally intensive encoded-index processing.
Consequently, the query-processing overhead of BVCRSA
increases only marginally as the query range expands.


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{fig_query_vs_N.png}
\caption{Query processing time versus database size.}
\label{fig:query_vs_database}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:query_vs_database} illustrates the scalability of query processing as the number of outsourced records increases from $1{,}000$ to $20{,}000$. BVCRSA consistently achieves the lowest query-processing latency, growing modestly from approximately $0.02$~ms to $0.45$~ms. In comparison, Trinity~\cite{ref26} and ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27} exhibit significantly higher latency, reaching approximately $180$~ms and $80$~ms, respectively, at the largest dataset size. Although Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28} and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} perform better than Trinity and ABSE-Range, their query times still exceed $2$~ms. The superior scalability of BVCRSA stems from its authenticated range-cover index and conjunctive bitmap-filtering mechanism, which enable direct identification of candidate intervals without extensive ciphertext scanning.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{fig_query_vs_d_conjunctive.png}
\caption{Query processing time versus number of queried dimensions.}
\label{fig:query_vs_dimension}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:query_vs_dimension} examines the scalability of conjunctive multidimensional queries by varying the number of queried dimensions from 1 to 5. BVCRSA exhibits only a gradual increase in query-processing latency, from approximately $0.04$~ms to $0.25$~ms. Although Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28} and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} maintain relatively stable performance across dimensions, their query-processing costs remain substantially higher at approximately $18$--$20$~ms and $3$~ms, respectively. This demonstrates that BVCRSA efficiently supports multidimensional range queries through dimension-level bitmap intersections, allowing conjunctive query evaluation with minimal additional overhead.

Overall, the results demonstrate that the combination of authenticated range-cover indexing and conjunctive bitmap filtering substantially reduces search overhead, enabling scalable and efficient multidimensional range-query processing for large-scale outsourced IIoT environments.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Query Throughput}}

This experiment evaluates the throughput of encrypted query processing to assess the efficiency of BVCRSA under increasing query workloads. The dataset size is fixed at $N=10{,}000$ records, while the number of submitted query requests varies from $100$ to $10{,}000$. Throughput is measured as

\[
\mathrm{Throughput}
=
\frac{Q_{\mathrm{completed}}}
     {T_{\mathrm{total}}},
\]

where $Q_{\mathrm{completed}}$ denotes the number of successfully processed queries and $T_{\mathrm{total}}$ denotes the total execution time.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{fig_query_throughput_matched_colors.png}
\caption{Query throughput under increasing query workload.}
\label{fig:query_throughput}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:query_throughput} compares the query throughput of BVCRSA with Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27}, Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}. BVCRSA consistently achieves the highest throughput across all workload sizes, significantly outperforming the baseline schemes. This improvement is attributed to the authenticated range-cover index and conjunctive bitmap-filtering mechanism, which enable efficient identification of matching records without extensive ciphertext scanning. In contrast, Trinity~\cite{ref26} and ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27} experience substantially lower throughput due to increasing index-traversal and cryptographic matching overhead as the workload grows. Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28} and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} achieve higher throughput than Trinity and ABSE-Range but remain constrained by lattice-based computations and pairing-based ciphertext testing, respectively.

Overall, the results demonstrate that BVCRSA sustains high query throughput under increasing workloads while maintaining low query latency. The combination of authenticated indexing and compact trapdoor evaluation makes BVCRSA well suited for large-scale IIoT environments where multiple users and applications continuously issue encrypted range queries.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Verification Overhead}}

This experiment evaluates the cost of verifying search-query results. The number of returned results is varied from $50$ to $500$, while the database size and query parameters remain fixed. The comparison includes BVCRSA, Trinity~\cite{ref26}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}, since these schemes provide explicit result-verification mechanisms. 
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{fig_verification_overhead.png}
\caption{Verification time versus number of returned results.}
\label{fig:verification_overhead}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:verification_overhead} compares the verification overhead of BVCRSA, Trinity~\cite{ref26}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} under varying result-set sizes. BVCRSA consistently achieves the lowest verification latency, increasing only from approximately $0.25$~ms to $1.65$~ms as the number of returned results grows from $50$ to $500$. This behavior is consistent with the proposed verification design, where lightweight Merkle-proof validation scales with the number of matched results while blockchain-root verification incurs only a constant overhead. In contrast, Trinity~\cite{ref26} exhibits a substantially higher and nearly linear increase in verification cost because its verification process requires validating authentication information associated with each returned result. VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} maintains an almost constant verification latency since verification is dominated by a fixed number of pairing-based operations. However, its verification cost remains consistently higher than that of BVCRSA due to the computational expense of bilinear pairings.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Effect of Homomorphic Aggregation}}

This experiment evaluates the scalability benefit of the proposed bitmap-constrained homomorphic aggregation mechanism. The number of matched records is varied from 100 to 500 while the query configuration remains fixed. We compare the full BVCRSA framework against a Naive baseline that returns all matched encrypted records to the user without performing in-network aggregation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{fig_ablation_aggregation.png}
\caption{Effect of homomorphic aggregation versus naive record retrieval.}
\label{fig:ablation_aggregation}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:ablation_aggregation} shows that the Naive approach incurs rapidly increasing latency as the number of matched records grows because all encrypted records must be transmitted and subsequently processed by the user. The execution time increases from approximately 180~ms to nearly 900~ms when the number of matched records reaches 500.

In contrast, BVCRSA performs aggregation directly over encrypted metadata maintained within the authenticated range-cover structure. Consequently, only compact aggregated ciphertexts and verification proofs are returned, substantially reducing communication and processing overhead. As a result, the execution time remains below 20~ms across all tested workloads and exhibits only a modest increase as the match volume grows.

These results demonstrate that the proposed homomorphic aggregation mechanism significantly improves query scalability while preserving data confidentiality and verifiability, making BVCRSA suitable for large-scale IIoT analytics workloads.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Aggregate-Recovery Scalability}}

This experiment evaluates the scalability of recovering
aggregate plaintexts using the Baby-Step Giant-Step (BSGS)
algorithm employed in the Threshold EC-ElGamal decryption
process. Since BSGS has
\(O(\sqrt{M_{\max}})\)
time complexity, where \(M_{\max}\) denotes the configured
maximum aggregate value, the recovery latency is expected to
increase sublinearly with the supported aggregation range.

The aggregate bound was varied from
\(10^{3}\) to \(10^{7}\), with each result averaged over
20 independent runs. As a baseline, we evaluated a
traditional EC-ElGamal implementation that recovers the
plaintext through naive linear search.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{bsgs_scalability.png}
\caption{Aggregate-recovery latency versus maximum
supported aggregate value.}
\label{fig:bsgs}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:bsgs} shows that the recovery latency of
BVCRSA increases gradually with the configured aggregate
bound, following the expected
\(O(\sqrt{M_{\max}})\)
behavior. In contrast, the traditional linear-search
baseline grows approximately linearly with the plaintext
domain, resulting in substantially higher recovery latency
as the aggregation range increases.

For the evaluated IIoT workload, the maximum possible
aggregate sum is
\(2\times10^{6}\),
which remains well within the configured BSGS search space.
Therefore, aggregate recovery incurs only a small fraction
of the overall query-processing time, demonstrating that
lifted Threshold EC-ElGamal remains practical for
large-scale IIoT monitoring applications.

\section{Discussion}

Although BVCRSA enables secure and authenticated
encrypted analytics over outsourced IIoT data, several
practical considerations remain.

First, BVCRSA is primarily designed for append-dominant
IIoT workloads. Frequent dynamic updates require
reconstruction of the affected authenticated range-cover
nodes, bitmap commitments, and Merkle paths. Although
these updates are localized, extremely high update rates
may increase maintenance overhead compared with relatively
static datasets.

Second, BVCRSA anchors only compact epoch commitments on
the blockchain rather than searchable indexes or encrypted
sensor data. Consequently, on-chain storage grows with the
number of finalized epochs instead of the dataset size.
Nevertheless, long-term deployments with short epoch
durations may gradually increase blockchain storage and
synchronization overhead.

Third, BVCRSA adopts a hierarchical architecture in which
lightweight sensors perform only data acquisition,
symmetric encryption, and authenticated metadata
generation, while computationally intensive operations are
offloaded to the Edge Gateway and cloud. This design
reduces the computational and memory requirements of
sensor devices, although highly resource-constrained edge
gateways may still require further optimization.
Physical tampering, denial-of-service, and side-channel
attacks are outside the scope of BVCRSA but can be
addressed through complementary deployment mechanisms,
such as secure boot, tamper-resistant hardware, intrusion
detection, and hardware-assisted side-channel
countermeasures.

Fourth, BVCRSA authenticates the consistency of the
returned query state by cryptographically binding the
canonical nodes, bitmap commitments, conjunctive bitmap,
aggregation ciphertexts, and blockchain-anchored
commitments. However, it does not provide general
verifiable computation of bitmap evaluation or
homomorphic aggregation, which would require additional
mechanisms such as zero-knowledge proofs, verifiable
computation, or trusted execution environments and would
introduce additional computation and communication
overhead.

Finally, BVCRSA follows the standard searchable-encryption
leakage model by revealing only predefined size, search,
and access patterns. Although this leakage is comparable
to that of representative searchable-encryption schemes,
it may still permit limited inference regarding query
selectivity or data distribution. Mitigating such leakage
through techniques such as ORAM or differential privacy
remains an important direction for future work.

\section{Conclusion}

This paper presented \textbf{BVCRSA}, a blockchain-based
framework for verifiable conjunctive range search and
aggregation over encrypted IIoT data. BVCRSA integrates
attribute-based searchable encryption, authenticated
hierarchical range-cover indexing, protected bitmap
filtering, Threshold EC-ElGamal homomorphic aggregation,
and blockchain-assisted query-state verification into a
unified architecture. Unlike existing searchable
encryption schemes that primarily support secure
retrieval, BVCRSA jointly enables fine-grained access
control, conjunctive multi-range search,
privacy-preserving aggregation, threshold aggregate
decryption, and authenticated query-state verification.
Experimental results demonstrate efficient trapdoor
generation, practical bitmap-based query processing, low
verification overhead, and scalable index construction,
highlighting the practicality of BVCRSA for secure
outsourced IIoT analytics. Future work will investigate
efficient revocation mechanisms, dynamic index updates,
large-scale evaluation using real-world IIoT datasets,
end-to-end performance under realistic deployment
conditions, distributed trust management, and
post-quantum cryptographic techniques to further enhance
the scalability, practicality, and long-term security of
the framework.

